[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Bridge Communication Mechanics and Serialization Bottlenecks in React Native"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Understanding how React Native communicates between the JavaScript thread and the Native (iOS/Android) thread is crucial for building high-performance applications. At the core of this architecture is the React Native Bridge, an asynchronous, serialized communication channel."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"How the Bridge Works"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The JavaScript thread and the Native UI thread run in separate execution contexts. They cannot share memory directly. Instead, messages must be serialized into JSON format, pushed across the bridge queue, and then deserialized on the receiving end."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Asynchronous nature: Native calls do not return values immediately to the JavaScript thread."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Batching: To minimize overhead, messages are frequently batched together and sent at the end of a frame or execution loop."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Serialization cost: Complex objects must be converted to strings via JSON.stringify() and parsed back on arrival."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Identifying Serialization Bottlenecks"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Serialization bottlenecks occur when too much data is sent across the bridge, or when data is sent too frequently. This causes CPU spikes on both threads, leading to dropped frames, sluggish touch responses, and UI jank."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"// Anti-pattern: Sending massive raw JSON payloads frequently across the bridge
const sendLargeDataToNative = (hugeDataSet) => {
  NativeModules.MyHeavyModule.processData(JSON.stringify(hugeDataSet));
};"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices for Mitigating Bridge Bottlenecks"},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Minimize cross-bridge traffic by keeping state local to the required thread when possible."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Pass identifiers or pointers instead of massive object graphs, fetching data directly on the native side if needed."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Throttle or debounce high-frequency events like scroll position updates, gestures, or layout measurements."},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Looking Forward: The New Architecture (JSI)"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"React Native's New Architecture replaces the asynchronous JSON bridge with the JavaScript Interface (JSI). The JSI allows JavaScript to hold direct references to C++ native objects and invoke native methods synchronously, completely eliminating serialization overhead and bridging delays."}]
